{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 19, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and a leader of the County of Tripoli a Crusader state in the Holy Land. He was born in 1140 and was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. *Early Life* Raymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli at the age of 12 after his father's assassination in 1152. As a result he was placed under the regency of his mother Hodierna who acted as regent until he came of age.\n\nRaymond III was taken captive by Nur ad-Din Zangi in 1164 during the Battle of Harim and was held prisoner for nearly 10 years. During his captivity *Regency in Antioch* he was regent for Bohemond III Prince of Antioch and his regency was marked by controversy and power struggles. He was eventually released in 1174 after a ransom was paid.\n\nUpon his return to Tripoli Raymond III found that the county was in a state of disarray and he set about restoring order and rebuilding its defenses. He also became involved in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and played a key role in the *Succession Crisis in Jerusalem* where he supported the claim of Raymond of Poitiers to the throne. However his efforts were ultimately unsuccessful and Guy of Lusignan was crowned king.\n\nRaymond III's later years were marked by a series of conflicts with Saladin who was seeking to unite the Muslim states of the region under his rule. Raymond III formed an alliance with the Ibelins and other nobles to counter Saladin's growing power but ultimately the alliance was unsuccessful. He died in 1187 without an heir and the County of Tripoli passed to his godson Raymond IV Count of Tripoli who was the son of Bohemond III Prince of Antioch. Raymond III's legacy was complex and multifaceted and he is remembered as one of the most important leaders of the Crusader states in the 12th century.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "lo